WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030
(upbeat music)

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:07.307
- We're pushing into a
12-plus-hour day and then some.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.420
- [Narrator] Dennis
Tajer is one of more than

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.220
a hundred thousand pilots in the US

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.980
grappling with this
summer's travel demand.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.990
It's led to chaotic scenes from long lines

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.500
at LaGuardia Airport to seas
of baggage in Frankfurt.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.500
- What I'm seeing right
now, I have never seen

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.860
in my 30 plus years at the airlines.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.240
- [Narrator] Tajer is also a spokesman

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.130
for the Allied Pilots Association,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.010
a union representing
American Airlines pilots.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.180
The two sides are currently

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.000
in the middle of negotiations
over a new contract.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.210
And Tajer says they're
understaffed and overwhelmed.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.080
- So there is this global
airline pilot shortage,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.570
and it's not something that can be quickly

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.193
or easily addressed.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.350
- [Narrator] While
travelers have returned,

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.720
there are 230,000 fewer workers

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.930
across the industry than there
were before the pandemic.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.850
We look at how all this is taking a toll

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.822
and how the industry is trying
to fix things for pilots.

00:00:59.822 --> 00:01:02.190
(plane roaring)

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.800
The root of the problem goes back to 2020.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.450
Lockdowns and travel restrictions due

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.690
to the pandemic stifled most
air travel around the world.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.900
To survive, the aviation industry
scaled back its workforce

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.030
with the four largest US carriers alone

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.060
losing more than 4,000 pilots in 2020.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.160
This shortage is being felt most acutely

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.660
by regional carriers,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.270
who've pulled out of some
routes with fewer passengers

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.970
restricting access to smaller cities.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.530
- Pilots are being stretched

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.950
at some airlines to their limits.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.330
- [Narrator] Henry
Harteveldt is the founder

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.470
of Atmosphere Research,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.023
an airline and travel
industry analysis group.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.170
He says that this shortage has upended

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.780
the control pilots have
over their schedules.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.343
Each month, airlines share
a roster of upcoming flights

00:01:45.343 --> 00:01:47.850
with pilots choosing the ones they want.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.520
Any remaining flights are
left up for grabs for pilots

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.930
that have some extra time.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.940
- They earn more money as a result

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.680
when they fly those trips.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.683
So it's a win-win for the
pilot and the airline.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:00.930
- [Narrator] But the predictability

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.940
of this system broke down in the summer

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.200
when the travel season was hit

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.680
by extreme weather, technical
glitches and other snafus.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.880
Flight schedules had very

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.270
little buffer room to deal
with these unexpected issues.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.993
- [Dennis] Some of 'em
are 10, 11 hour days.

00:02:14.993 --> 00:02:17.200
That's basically gonna give me about

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.853
little over an hour of buffer.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.430
- [Narrator] And the
resulting fight delays

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.820
and cancellations left both
passengers and staff uncertain

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.100
about when they would be flying.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.170
This unpredictability has made some pilots

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.480
hesitant to take extra flights.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.693
- There is a deep resistance
to actually go pick up time.

00:02:36.693 --> 00:02:39.607
Once you're in the system,
they come in and say,

00:02:39.607 --> 00:02:41.070
"Guess what? You're not going home.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.259
You're gonna go for another day or two."

00:02:43.259 --> 00:02:45.420
- [Narrator] To manage a
challenge facing pilots

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.150
as well as other staffing
shortages across the industry,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.000
some airlines are
adjusting their scheduling.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.580
American Airlines said during
its July earnings call,

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.290
that it would reduce scheduled flights

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.170
for at least the rest of the year.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:01.260
- [Robert] We pulled out
some additional flying

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.080
and that's flying that we would rather do.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.790
- [Narrator] This is a
painful decision to make

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.100
after the industry spent
about two years waiting

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.750
for demand to return.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.570
- Obviously this comes at a cost.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.580
For one thing, they
still have all the pilots

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.650
that they have on payroll.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.810
They've got the lease or debt payment

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.090
to make on all of those aircraft.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.630
And they have all of the
other employees on staff

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.550
who have to be paid.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.950
- [Narrator] The cutting flights
won't fix another problem.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.130
Quickly replacing the pilots
lost during the pandemic.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:36.303
- It's incredibly difficult
to get these wings.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.960
- [Narrator] Pilots have to go
through a wide range of tests

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.650
and licensing requirements,
as well as fly for 1500 hours

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.320
before they can start working
for a commercial airline.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.580
- [Dennis] A hundred
thousand dollars just to

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.980
get the certification,
let alone the amount

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.560
of time and money you'll
have to spend to get

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.720
the experience to move forward.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.370
- [Narrator] Aviation
analysts and flight schools

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.010
in the US say, it can take
up to two to four years

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.320
to get the required
training and experience.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.750
- These young pilots coming through,

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.510
some of them they have to
provide for their families

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.330
while they're flying these in-between jobs

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.700
to get that experience.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.800
- [Narrator] To make this
process easier American Airlines

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.630
said it's focusing on removing training

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.610
and financial barriers for its aviators

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.848
through its Cadet Academy and
faster training will be key,

00:04:20.848 --> 00:04:24.180
because 2022 is already
the busiest year on record

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.500
for pilot hiring.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.050
Major US carriers are
also on track to hire

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.060
more than 10,000 of them this year

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.520
according to one pilot advisory firm.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.320
As of August, American Airlines alone

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.170
had hired over 1,200 pilots.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.780
- The airlines are not doing nothing.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.400
They're doing all they can.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.993
The challenge is that they
need to be doing more.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.290
- [Narrator] As the summer
travel surge begins to fade,

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.690
airlines are hoping to
avoid the same snafus

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.730
during the winter holiday season.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:56.340
American Airlines said the
reduced flight schedule

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.590
in the fall will give
them space to prepare

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.390
for the end of this year.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.880
- [Vasu] And then as we go
forward into later September,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.700
October, and deeper into the winter,

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.493
we have time yet to go and
figure out how that goes.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:11.880
- [Narrator] But it
may be too late by then

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.790
as the summer experience
has put off some customers.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.519
One survey found that 17%

00:05:16.519 --> 00:05:19.530
of leisure travelers said
they're less likely to fly

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:20.730
over Christmas and New Year

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.430
because of airline
disruptions during the summer.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.800
- This is what we are
alarmed by right now,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.370
less costing and a cost
isn't just in money.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.790
The cost is in the trust.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:34.457
(gentle music)

